WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.610
We'll now return to the questions that we

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.380
asked at the very beginning of this
lesson.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:12.540
Starting from genomes, how can we
transform them into synteny blocks?

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.790
And the first thing I want you to learn is
the notation of genomic dot plot.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:24.270
Let's arrange genome along x coordinate
and along y coordinate.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.010
And let's represent

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:31.190
every k-mer that is shed at position x and
y in the

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:37.670
genome as a dot, a position with
coordinates x and y.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:42.539
In this case, I show you genomic dot plot
for k-mer size equal to 3.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:47.040
And it only consists of the diagonal in
the genomic dot plot.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.675
Of course, diagonal is always present in
the genomic

00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:57.786
dot plot because k-mers at the coordinate
x and x are always the same.

00:00:57.786 --> 00:01:04.086
But here's the genomic dot plot for k=2,
and you can see that in

00:01:04.086 --> 00:01:10.280
this case the number of dots increased as
compared to k=3.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:16.790
For example these 4 dots correspond to
repeated occurrences of the 2-mer CT in

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.890
the genome.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.450
So far, we talked about a genome
consisting

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:30.740
of one strand, but in reality of course
genome consists of 2 strands,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:35.710
and therefore we should take care of both
identical and

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:40.610
reverse complementary k-mers while
representing genomic dot plots,

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:45.480
as shown here.
In this case, red points correspond to

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.065
identical k-mers, and blue points
correspond to

00:01:50.065 --> 00:01:55.550
reverse complementary k-mers.
Now

00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:01.140
this is how a genomic dot plot looks like
when we compare genome against itself.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.000
But can we also construct genomic

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.112
dot plots when comparing two different
genomes?

00:02:06.112 --> 00:02:11.115
And here's an example of comparing one
genome with another

00:02:11.115 --> 00:02:15.642
one that is obtained from the first one by
a reversal.

00:02:15.642 --> 00:02:20.710
And in this case, you can see that a
reversal corresponds

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:26.560
to the -45 degree diagonal formed by blue

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.340
points in this genomic dot plot.
Now, from

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.230
looking at the small string, let's now
construct genomic dot

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:41.055
plot for entire E. Coli
and Salmonella genomes.

00:02:41.055 --> 00:02:46.620
And human eye immediately see synteny
blocks

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:52.710
in this genomic dot plot, they simply
correspond to various diagonals.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:56.890
And we only pay attention to sufficiently
long diagonal, because small

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.570
diagonal, or small points, clusters of
points may represent noise in

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.700
this case.
So we see five synteny blocks, five

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:11.030
diagonal corresponding to a block marked
asa a, b, c, d and e,

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:16.690
and if we project these blocks on the

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:22.870
x axis we will get an arrangement of
synteny blocks in E. Coli.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.650
And if we project them on the y-axis then
we'll get

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.650
an arrangement of synteny blocks in
salmonella.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:36.434
And now we are ready to analyze the
2-break distance between these two genomes.

00:03:36.434 --> 00:03:41.870
Therefore, construction of synteny blocks
essentially amount

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:46.460
to constructing diagonals in the genomic
dot plot.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.030
And here is the finding synteny blocks
problem.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.870
The input is the set of points in

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.250
genomic dot plots in 2D, and the output is

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:01.560
a set of diagonals in DotPlot representing
synteny blocks.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.300
Does it make sense?

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.240
Of course it doesn't make sense.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.260
Because I have not defined what diagonals.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.670
Look at this genomic dot plot and you can
see the diagonals

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:17.950
are not perfect because there are many
insertions, deletions, mismatches and many

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.630
other artifacts, and therefore human eye
has the ability

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.890
to somehow process this genomic dot plot
into

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.410
five diagonals, but we really do not know

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.620
what our brain is doing while making this
transformation.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.038
Can we come up with an algorithm for
constructing these diagonals?

00:04:37.038 --> 00:04:43.260
Let's try.
Let's look at the genomic dot plot between

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.000
two different genomes that we constructed
before.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.740
And let's represent every dot in the dot
plot as a node in the graph.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:05:00.630
And we will form edges in this graph by
simply connecting closely located points.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.890
For example, points that are located at a

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:08.230
distance less than maxDistance, where
maxDistance is a parameter.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:09.500
What will happen?

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:15.150
We will connect certain points by edges,
and you can see from here

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:21.430
that diagonals actually correspond to
connected components in this graph.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.110
Moreover, we probably should ignore very
small connected components.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.570
For example, a component located in the
upper-right (upper-left to the viewer)

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:33.550
corner and low left corners (lower-right to the viewer),
but the three remaining

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:39.410
components should be accepted as synteny
blocks in our genome.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:46.720
And therefore, I offer you the following
algorithm for solving the problem.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.945
Let's form a graph whose node set is the
set of points in DotPlot, and

00:05:51.945 --> 00:05:54.550
connect two nodes by an edge if the

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:59.280
corresponding nodes are located at close
distance from another.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.040
The connected components in the resulting
graph define synteny block.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.250
And afterwards, delete small synteny
blocks.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:12.800
Of course this is not the only algorithm
that tries to model what the human eye is

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.590
doing, while clustering points in 2D, and
our brain is very good at clustering.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.440
I can give you another algorithm, for
example this one that I call Amalgamate.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.570
And it actually different, but it looks
like it's doing almost the same thing.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:33.011
First step, define each point in DotPlot
as

00:06:33.011 --> 00:06:39.030
a separate block and iteratively
amalgamate the resulting block.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.170
How do we amalgamate the blocks?

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.680
We amalgamate two blocks and combine them
into a single block

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:49.590
if they contain two points that are
separated by small distance in

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:53.330
another genome, and as before delete small
synteny blocks.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.620
So we have a problem and we have two
algorithms for solving it.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.410
Which algorithm would you use?

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.320
Instead of answering this question, you
should

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.830
ask me what problem I actually want you

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.900
to solve, because I have never defined an

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.770
algorithmic problem that I want you to
solve.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:18.544
You should have refused coming up with an
algorithm

00:07:18.544 --> 00:07:23.790
before actually seeing the problem I want
to address.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:27.640
Unfortunately, in bioinformatics, it's not
as simple.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:33.100
I have been working on the problem of
synteny block generation for 10 years now,

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.320
and I failed to formulate this problem as

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.920
a rigorous algorithmic problem, and that's
why we

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.890
are at mercy of our intuition.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:46.860
And some people may like Synteny algorithm
better.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.350
Other people may like Amalgamate algorithm
better.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:51.140
How would we choose?

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:57.120
The choice should be done based on the
practical results.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.990
And if you implement these algorithms and
run them, then you

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.755
see that synteny algorithm gives

00:08:02.755 --> 00:08:06.410
a reasonable and biologically adequate
result.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:09.855
But amalgamate algorithm, also, it looks
reasonable, it looks like it

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:15.450
models what human eye may be doing, gives
completely wrong results.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.170
After developing the algorithm for
constructing

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.190
synteny blocks, we are now ready to

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:26.070
find out what are the synteny blocks
between human and mouse X chromosome.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:30.850
And here's a genomic dot plot for the X
chromosome consisting of 25,000 dots.

00:08:32.138 --> 00:08:38.782
We form a synteny graph on these 25,000
nodes.

00:08:38.782 --> 00:08:43.175
And at this slide, you can see the resulting
connected components.

00:08:43.175 --> 00:08:48.627
We further process of these connected
components and represent them

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:54.900
as a perfect +45 degree or -45
degree diagonals in 2-D.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.400
Where are the 11 synteny

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.580
blocks between human and mouse that we
have been considering in this lecture?

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.710
Well if you project these 11 blocks in 2D
to x-axis,

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:12.990
you will get the arrangement of human
synteny blocks.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.480
And if you project it to y-axis, then

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.367
you will get the arrangement of mouse
synteny blocks.

00:09:19.367 --> 00:09:22.607
Moreover, since we are not particularly
interested

00:09:22.607 --> 00:09:25.991
in the scale of these blocks, and even if some
blocks appear

00:09:25.991 --> 00:09:29.913
small, there are maybe hundreds of genes
in each of the blocks.

00:09:29.913 --> 00:09:31.154
It's just an issue of scale.

00:09:31.154 --> 00:09:37.687
So let us now represent each block by the
diagonal of the same size.

00:09:37.687 --> 00:09:42.247
And once again, if we project these blocks
into 2D to the

00:09:42.247 --> 00:09:47.662
x axis, we will get an arrangement of synteny
blocks in Phillip, and if

00:09:47.662 --> 00:09:54.177
we project it to y-axis, we will get the
arrangement of synteny blocks in mouse.

00:09:54.177 --> 00:09:57.687
And now, after we constructed synteny
blocks for two

00:09:57.687 --> 00:10:02.850
genomes, How about constructing synteny
blocks for three genomes?

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.620
You can think about generalizing how

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.650
a synteny block construction approach for
more

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.760
than two genomes, but what about

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:13.160
constructing synteny blocks for 100
genomes that

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:16.179
are being sequenced now?

00:10:16.179 --> 00:10:19.730
This actually turns out to be a very
difficult problem.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:26.330
And nobody in the world today is able to
construct synteny blocks for 100 genomes.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:30.810
Maybe after this course you will suggest a
reasonable approach for this.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:34.540
But an interesting thing is that
if we learn how

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:38.250
to construct synteny blocks for many
genomes, we will

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.150
be able to come up with a very

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:47.400
accurate picture of chromosomal
organization in our distant ancestor.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.220
So there is lot of work

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:52.250
ahead in developing genome rearrangement
and synteny

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:59.350
block construction algorithms, but this is
already beyond the scope of this course.

